content.thinking:
Confirmed: all four edge cases crash. The bug report's case (white pawn on h8) reproduces.

Now the fix. The root cause is in `_moves`: the single-square non-capturing pawn push lacks the `0x88` off-board check. When a pawn sits on its last rank (white on rank 8, black on rank 1), the push goes off-board and creates a move to an invalid square, which later crashes `_pieceKey` with a BigInt type error.

Minimal fix: add `0x88` check to the single push, matching the capture-loop style:

```ts
// single square, non-capturing
to = from + PAWN_OFFSETS[us][0]
if (!(to & 0x88) && !this._board[to]) {
  addMove(moves, us, from, to, PAWN)

  // double square
  to = from + PAWN_OFFSETS[us][1]
  if (SECOND_RANK[us] === rank(from) && !this._board[to]) {
    addMove(moves, us, from, to, PAWN, undefined, BITS.BIG_PAWN)
  }
}
```

Should I also guard the double push? A pawn on SECOND_RANK has single push always on-board (rank 2 → rank 3/4, all within board). So the double push is never off-board when it can actually trigger. But for defensive consistency, the double push check `!this._board[to]` with `to` off-board would also be a problem if it could trigger... it can't, since it only runs if `SECOND_RANK[us] === rank(from)`, and rank(from) is only rank 2 for white / rank 7 for black, where both single and double pushes stay on the board.

Actually wait, I should double check the double push: white on rank 2 (indices 96-103). to = from - 32 = indices 64-71 → rank 4. On board. Black on rank 7 (indices 16-23). to = from + 32 = 48-55 → rank 5. On board. Good.

So the minimal fix is just the single push check.

Now let me also check whether there might be an issue in `_moveFromSan` for strict vs non-strict... no, the root cause is clear.

Let me apply the fix.

